    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.59887/2073-6673.2025.19(1)-7",
    "abstract": "The review systematizes research from the last decade on modeling the non-radioactive impact of nuclear power plants (NPPs) on cooling water bodies. It examines modern approaches to numerical modeling, including adapted hydrodynamic models and original domestic developments for assessing thermal and chemical impacts. Based on the analysis, promising directions for the development of modeling tools in this field are formulated, such as: incorporating feedback with atmospheric processes to improve forecast accuracy in coastal zones with complex wind circulation; developing models of interaction with bottom sediments; integrating with ecological risk models to transition from assessing abiotic parameters to direct forecasting of impacts on specific species and populations of aquatic organisms; creating detailed forecasts for extreme hydrometeorological scenarios under changing climate conditions to assess ecosystem resilience; and actively applying models at pre-investment stages of designing new NPPs to optimize the siting and configuration of discharge and water intake structures to minimize potential environmental impact.",
    "field": "Modeling of non-radioactive pollution from nuclear power plants",
    "problem": "This review addresses the need to advance numerical models for predicting non-radioactive NPP impacts by integrating complex factors like atmospheric feedback, sediment interactions, and ecosystem responses to move beyond simple assessments of abiotic parameters.",
    "method": "This review analyzes the use of integrated three-dimensional baroclinic hydrodynamic and biogeochemical models, validated with remote sensing data, to assess the ecological impacts of non-radioactive thermal and chemical pollution from nuclear power plant discharge waters on cooling ponds.",
    "task": "The task is to systematically review research from the last decade on modeling the non-radioactive impact of nuclear power plant discharge on cooling water bodies to identify modern approaches, trends, and future development directions for these models.",
